= Staal Considers the Assembly

Staal has governed by decree since the day he took the chancellery and has never pretended to regret it. But within the <span style="color: #BFC8CC;">**GNU**</span> a different argument is gaining ground: that a tamed Assembly is worth more than a silent one. A majority of the right, formally seated and formally voting, would give the government a legitimacy that no Emergency Article can provide, at home and in the eyes of the powers watching from the north.

Staal is unconvinced but not unpersuadable. The question, he is reported to have said, is not whether the chamber would obey but whether the chamber is worth the feeding.

Word reaches us that, if the numbers allow it after the next election, the general may accept a parliamentary coalition of the right rather than rule against the chamber. For the Assembly it would be survival of a kind, and domestication of another.
